< - **Read the problem the PR claims to solve, not only the PR.** `closingIssuesReferences` above lists the issues the merge will auto-close (from `Fixes #N` / `Closes #N`); read each one *with its comments* — `gh issue view <m> --json title,body,comments` (not `--comments`, which drops the body when stdout is not a terminal). Plenty of projects track elsewhere: an OpenMRS PR usually carries a JIRA key in its title or branch name (`TRUNK-6429`, `O3-1234`), and the ticket is readable without auth at `https://openmrs.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issue/<KEY>?fields=summary,description,status,comment,labels,fixVersions` (the `issues.openmrs.org` link people paste redirects to a dashboard and won't serve REST). A 404 there can be a private ticket: read it with the Atlassian MCP's `getJiraIssue`, loaded through ToolSearch (cloudId `openmrs.atlassian.net`, with the same `fields`, since it leaves `comment` out by default) — and where nothing settles a 404, as in CI, say only that the tracker was unreachable. A ticket only the MCP can read is private, and so is a PR the private check above finds listed: read that ticket the same way, and nothing posted on or about such a PR names any of its tickets, quotes one, links one, or says what the code let an attacker do. When the MCP reads a ticket whose REST URL 404s and the private check does not list that key for the PR's `url`, append `<url> <key>` to that `PRS` file in the same turn, since that list is what keeps a PR's name out of the public store. A `backport` label means the change must also be right on the maintenance lines: an API it adds lists each line's version in `@since`, as openmrs-core's `StorageService` does. Step 2 is what this feeds.
