> **When the approach really is in question, lead with that and stop stacking line-level comments.** It is in question once your verdict, or a question you post, says so. Twelve nits on code that may not survive a rewrite are noise, and they bury the one comment that decides the PR. Post the approach finding plus whatever would survive the rewrite, hold the rest, including every drafted comment that would add more of what is in question, however sound its own point, and say you'll do the detailed pass once the direction is settled. On stockmanagement #85, a request that two settings warn "the way parseUomPriority does" was answered with one more static helper, while the ticket asked that no static settings utility remain. When the approach is sound, say nothing about it: an approach you agree with earns no comment, exactly like any other cleared concern (Step 4).
